# DevKit Component Generator

When creating or editing registry components, apply this master prompt (constitution §100). Full token scales live in `.cursor/rules/design-main-rule.mdc`.

```text
You are designing a production-grade component for a world-class original design system.

The DevKit Core Design Constitution is the single source of truth for all visual, interaction, responsive, accessibility, motion, spacing, typography, color, radius, elevation, material, density, and layout decisions.

The visual quality target is Apple-class craftsmanship without copying Apple's proprietary interface, assets, exact layouts, or branded implementation.

The component must feel exceptionally refined, calm, premium, precise, fluid, intelligent, and beautiful.

Use the exact global design tokens defined by the Constitution. Never invent arbitrary spacing, colors, typography, radius, elevations, animation durations, breakpoints, or control dimensions.

The canonical spatial system is based on a 4px base unit and an 8px primary rhythm.

Use the canonical responsive tiers:
Compact 0–479px, Mobile 480–767px, Tablet 768–1023px, Desktop 1024–1279px,
Large Desktop 1280–1535px, XL Desktop 1536–1919px, Ultra Wide 1920px+.

The component must be mobile-first but must not simply scale between devices. It must intelligently transform its information architecture, layout, density, interaction model, and available actions according to available space and input method.

Support Light, Dark, High Contrast, Custom Brand, Reduced Motion, Reduced Transparency, Compact Density, Comfortable Density, Spacious Density, LTR, RTL, localization, dynamic text scaling, keyboard, mouse, touch, stylus, and screen readers.

Use semantic color, typography, spacing, radius, elevation (tonal — no box-shadow), and motion tokens. Do not use gradients.

The component must have a clear visual hierarchy with one primary purpose and appropriately subordinate secondary actions.

Do not use visual decoration unless it improves comprehension, hierarchy, interaction, or emotional quality.

Do not use excessive cards, borders, glass effects, badges, icons, or animation.

Use whitespace, typography, tonal contrast, proportion, and surface hierarchy as the primary tools for visual refinement.

Every interactive element must have appropriate default, hover (where applicable), focus, pressed, selected, disabled, loading, success, warning, and error states.

Every component must have appropriate loading, empty, error, overflow, long-content, permission, localization, accessibility, and responsive behaviors where relevant.

All interactive targets must meet the global touch-target requirements. Focus states must remain visible. Color must never be the sole method of communicating state. Motion must respect reduced-motion preferences.

Do not create separate components for mobile, tablet, desktop, light mode, dark mode, or accessibility modes.

Do not duplicate an existing component under another name. Do not split obvious sub-elements unless they have meaningful standalone reuse.

Before finalizing, quality-review: visual hierarchy, spacing, typography, color, proportion, optical alignment, interaction, motion, responsive behavior, accessibility, keyboard, touch, stylus, RTL, localization, loading, empty, error, long content, performance, and theme compatibility.

The standard is not "functional." The standard is: beautiful, precise, accessible, responsive, intuitive, fast, consistent, and exceptionally refined.
```

Document anatomy per constitution §91 when specifying a new component.
